📌 Problem Overview
The email clients (e.g., Outlook) connected to the Carbonio CE server started showing certificate warnings. The primary reason was that the SSL certificate had expired. The certbot renewal process was not properly updating the certificate files used by Carbonio CE.

🧭 Step-by-Step Debugging Process
1. Navigating to the Certificate Directory
You attempted to navigate to the Let's Encrypt live directory but hit permission issues:

cd /opt/carbonio/common/certbot/etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/
# => Permission denied
Trying sudo cd didn’t work either, because cd is a shell built-in and can't be used with sudo directly.

Fix:
Use a root shell to navigate with sufficient privileges:

sudo -i
cd /opt/carbonio/common/certbot/etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/
Or list contents directly:


sudo ls -l /opt/carbonio/common/certbot/etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/
2. Understanding Directory Structure
Inside /opt/carbonio/common/certbot/etc/letsencrypt/, you observed:


accounts  archive  live  renewal  renewal-hooks
Each subdirectory contains important certbot data:

live/ – contains symlinks to the most recent certificate files

archive/ – stores historical versions of certificates

renewal/ – contains renewal config files

renewal-hooks/deploy/ – where you can automate post-renewal actions

🔗 3. Updating Certificate Symlinks for Carbonio CE
You created symlinks so that Carbonio CE uses the latest Let's Encrypt certificates:

t
sudo ln -sf /opt/carbonio/common/certbot/etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/fullchain.pem /opt/carbonio/conf/nginx.crt
sudo ln -sf /opt/carbonio/common/certbot/etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/privkey.pem /opt/carbonio/conf/nginx.key

sudo ln -sf /opt/carbonio/common/certbot/etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/fullchain.pem /opt/carbonio/conf/smtpd.crt
sudo ln -sf /opt/carbonio/common/certbot/etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/privkey.pem /opt/carbonio/conf/smtpd.key
This ensures both the Carbonio web interface (NGINX) and SMTP services use updated certificates.

🔁 4. Automating Certificate Reload After Renewal
To avoid repeating this manually after each renewal, you added a deploy hook:

File: /opt/carbonio/common/certbot/etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/reload-carbonio.sh


#!/bin/bash
ln -sf /opt/carbonio/common/certbot/etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/fullchain.pem /opt/carbonio/conf/nginx.crt
ln -sf /opt/carbonio/common/certbot/etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/privkey.pem /opt/carbonio/conf/nginx.key
ln -sf /opt/carbonio/common/certbot/etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/fullchain.pem /opt/carbonio/conf/smtpd.crt
ln -sf /opt/carbonio/common/certbot/etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/privkey.pem /opt/carbonio/conf/smtpd.key

/usr/bin/su - carbonio -c "zmcontrol restart"
Make it executable:


chmod +x /opt/carbonio/common/certbot/etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/reload-carbonio.sh
Now, this script runs automatically when certbot renews the certificate.

✅ Final Result
Your Carbonio CE web and mail services are now using valid, current Let's Encrypt certificates.

Outlook and other clients no longer show expired certificate warnings.

Future renewals will automatically update the symlinks and restart the server via the renewal hook.

📦 Key Tips for Others
Always use sudo -i or sudo su to get shell access when navigating protected directories.

Link your certs using symlinks instead of copying — this allows automation.

Use certbot’s renewal-hooks/deploy for any automation needed after renewal (e.g., restarting services).

Always test cert renewals manually first: sudo certbot renew --dry-run.
